  "question": {
    "text": "The value of \\(a\\) in order that \\(f(x)=\\sin x-\\cos x-a x+b\\) decreases for all real values is given by",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "2oCyr",
      "text": "\\(a \\geq \\sqrt{2}\\)",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "6nZVC",
      "text": "a \\(  < \\sqrt{2}\\)",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "ZQme5",
      "text": "\\(a \\geq 1\\)",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "0SoXo",
      "text": "\\(a  < 1\\)",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "We have; \\(f(x)=\\sin x-\\cos x-a x+b\\)\n\\(\\Rightarrow f^{\\prime}(x)=\\cos x+\\sin x-a\\)\n\\(\\Rightarrow \\mathrm{f}^{\\prime}(\\mathrm{x})  < 0 ~\\forall~ \\mathrm{x} \\in \\mathrm{R}\\)\n\\(\\Rightarrow(\\cos x+\\sin x) < a ~\\forall~ x \\in R\\)\nAs the max. value of \\((\\cos x+\\sin x)\\) is \\(\\sqrt{2}\\)\nThe above is possible when \\(a \\geq \\sqrt{2}\\)",
    "image": null
  },
